{"step_index":94,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-01T11:40:09Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-01T17:10:09+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-10-01T17:10:16+05:30\nThe search for \"can you use NVIDIA external GPU eGPU with MacBook Air M5 Apple Silicon 2025 2026 Thunderbolt\" returned the following summary:\nNo, you cannot use an NVIDIA external GPU (eGPU) with your MacBook Air M5 for standard tasks like gaming, video editing, or external display acceleration[1][2][3].\n\nApple Silicon Macs, including those with the M5 chip, do not provide the necessary driver support or architecture to use eGPUs as graphics accelerators[2][3].\n\n### Key Details to Understand:\n*   **No Graphics Acceleration:** Unlike older Intel-based Macs, Apple Silicon does not support Metal, OpenGL, or OpenCL acceleration via external GPUs[4][1][3]. You cannot plug in an eGPU to improve frame rates in games or speed up rendering in professional creative apps[1][2].\n*   **The \"TinyGPU\" Exception:** You may have seen reports about a project called \"TinyGPU\"[5][6]. In 2026, a driver was released that allows for basic communication between NVIDIA/AMD cards and Apple Silicon Macs, but it is **strictly for compute tasks** (such as running AI Large Language Models in Docker)[1][7][8]. It does not provide graphics output or gaming acceleration[1][9].\n*   **Architectural Limitations:** The M-series chips use a \"Unified Memory Architecture\" (UMA) where the GPU is tightly integrated into the System on a Chip (SoC)[2][3]. Apple’s design philosophy for these chips is centered on this integrated efficiency, and they have not enabled support for external graphics cards as they did in the past[2][3].\n\n**Summary:** If your goal is to boost your MacBook Air's performance for gaming or creative work, an eGPU is not a viable solution[2][3]. The only use case for an external GPU setup on a modern Mac is highly specific AI research/compute work, which is complex to set up and d